// ============================================================================
// LiquidityFlowAuse — Structure Break Module
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Module:   Structure Break (BoS/ChoCh)
// Version:  LiquidityFlowAuse v1.0.0
//
// Purpose:  Tracks confirmed 1H swing highs and lows, detects a break of the
//           most recent opposite swing by CLOSE, classifies that break as
//           continuation (BoS) or reversal (ChoCh) against the structure in
//           force, persists the structure bias, and renders the swing levels
//           and break markers.
//
// THE CENTRAL INVARIANT: structureFlipped IS A PROPERTY OF A BREAK, NOT A
// SECOND BREAK. ChoCh is BoS plus a structure precondition — a SUBSET, not a
// sibling. An earlier draft exposed bullishBoS and bullishChoCh as independent
// booleans, and spec 7.3 then awarded +20 for any break and a further +30 for
// a ChoCh, so every reversal break scored 50 points for one event. Do not
// reintroduce parallel flags; a consumer that wants "structure turned
// bullish" tests `breakUp and structureFlipped`.
//
// Pine:     Pine Script v5. The //@version=5 directive is declared once in
//           the main indicator file (src/liquidityflowause.pine); this
//           module MUST NOT contain an indicator() or study() declaration.
//
// Depends:  Nothing from the earlier modules. The pivots this module needs are
//           fetched here under the sb_ prefix — see the mandatory note on the
//           1H fetch below for why they cannot be shared with Liquidity Zones.
//
// Usage:    Designed for file concatenation into the main indicator file.
//           For a standalone syntax check in the Pine Editor, wrap the
//           contents of this file in a temporary indicator() declaration.
// ============================================================================

// ─── User Inputs ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Structure pivot length. DELIBERATELY DIFFERENT from Liquidity Zones'
// pivotLenHigh / pivotLenLow (default 10). A shorter length here describes
// finer-grained 1H structure: the swings a break is measured against, not the
// swing points liquidity is expected to pool behind. They are two different
// descriptions of the same price, so they are two different inputs.
structPivotLen = input.int(5, "Structure Pivot Length", minval=3, group="Structure Break")

// Marker toggle. Separate from the level toggle below because the LEVELS are
// context (where price is relative to the last swing) while the MARKERS are
// events (a break happened) — useful to want one without the other.
showStructureBreaks = input.bool(true, "Show Structure Breaks", group="Structure Break")
showSwingLevels     = input.bool(true, "Show Swing Levels",     group="Structure Break")

// ─── 1H Swing Fetch ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE sb_ PREFIX IS MANDATORY, NOT COSMETIC. liquidity-zones.pine already
// declares h1_pivotHigh / h1_pivotLow at module scope, and the modules are
// concatenated into a single script, so reusing those names here is a duplicate
// declaration and a compile error.
//
// They could not be shared even if naming permitted it: Liquidity Zones uses
// pivotLenHigh / pivotLenLow (default 10) to place tier-3 liquidity zones,
// while this module uses structPivotLen (default 5) to define the structure
// that a break is measured against. Different lengths describe different
// structure, so they must be different variables.
//
// NO [1] OFFSET. ta.pivothigh() / ta.pivotlow() are intrinsically
// non-repainting — they return a value only on the bar where the pivot is
// confirmed, and na afterwards. Combined with lookahead=barmerge.lookahead_off
// that is sufficient on its own. The [1] pattern in spec 2.2 applies to direct
// OHLC access, where the previously confirmed bar is specifically wanted;
// adding one here would delay every swing by a full extra 1H bar for no gain.
// The same reasoning as the identical block in liquidity-zones.pine.

sb_pivotHigh = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, "60", ta.pivothigh(high, structPivotLen, structPivotLen), lookahead=barmerge.lookahead_off)
sb_pivotLow  = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, "60", ta.pivotlow(low,  structPivotLen, structPivotLen),  lookahead=barmerge.lookahead_off)

// ─── Swing State (spec 6.3) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Persistent across bars via var. lastSwingHigh / lastSwingLow are the LEVELS a
// break is measured against; the Bar fields record WHEN each was confirmed so a
// level line can be anchored at its pivot bar.
//
// ALL MUTATION IS AT GLOBAL SCOPE BY DESIGN. There is no array here, so this is
// not the array-mutation question the other two modules answer, but the choice
// is the same one: every write to a `var` is visible in this one block instead
// of inside a call graph. (For the record, Pine v5 functions CAN reassign
// globals too, so a helper here would compile — it is a readability choice,
// not a language limitation.)
//
// prevSwingHigh / prevSwingLow hold the level BEFORE the most recent
// confirmation. Nothing in this module or in the Signal Engine reads them yet;
// they are kept because spec 6.3 declares them and a future Fractal-structure
// variant consumes the pair. They are inert state today, not a live signal.

var float lastSwingHigh = na
var float lastSwingLow  = na
var float prevSwingHigh = na
var float prevSwingLow  = na
var int   lastSwingHighBar = na
var int   lastSwingLowBar  = na

// Structure in force: 1 bullish, -1 bearish, 0 not yet established. Declared
// here, next to the swing state it is derived from, so the break block below
// can both read it and write it. `var` — it PERSISTS across bars and is part
// of this module's exported interface (spec 6.7).
var int marketStructure = 0

// A confirmed pivot supersedes the previous level of the same kind.
//
// The Bar field is stamped with bar_index, i.e. the bar the pivot was
// CONFIRMED on, not the bar the pivot itself sits on. The pivot sits
// structPivotLen bars in the past, and this level did not exist — was not
// knowable, and could not have been broken — until the confirmation bar. The
// level line is therefore anchored where the level became tradeable, which is
// also the only anchor available without a second request.security() fetch to
// recover the pivot bar.

if not na(sb_pivotHigh)
    prevSwingHigh   := lastSwingHigh
    lastSwingHigh   := sb_pivotHigh
    lastSwingHighBar := bar_index

if not na(sb_pivotLow)
    prevSwingLow   := lastSwingLow
    lastSwingLow   := sb_pivotLow
    lastSwingLowBar := bar_index

// ─── Break Detection and Module Outputs (spec 6.4 / 6.7) ────────────────────
//
//   breakUp           price crossed ABOVE the last confirmed 1H swing high
//   breakDown         price crossed BELOW the last confirmed 1H swing low
//   structureFlipped  that break opposed the structure in force → ChoCh
//   marketStructure   1 bullish, -1 bearish, 0 not yet established
//
// The first three are recomputed each bar, so no var. marketStructure is a var
// declared above and persists. All four are declared before the block that
// assigns three of them, and are read by the Signal Engine after this module is
// spliced in.
//
// A BREAK IS A CROSS, NOT A THRESHOLD. The condition needs the level held on
// the previous bar and lost on this one. Testing `close` rather than high /
// low is deliberate: a wick through a level that is immediately rejected is
// liquidity being taken, which is the Liquidity Zones module's job. Using the
// bar's extreme here would have both modules reporting the same event.
//
// The na guard is required, not defensive: lastSwingHigh is na until the first
// pivot is confirmed, and every comparison against na is na, which would leave
// structureFlipped indeterminate rather than false.

bool breakUp         = false
bool breakDown       = false
bool structureFlipped = false

bool crossedAbove = not na(lastSwingHigh) and close >  lastSwingHigh and close[1] <= lastSwingHigh
bool crossedBelow = not na(lastSwingLow)  and close <  lastSwingLow  and close[1] >= lastSwingLow

// REVERSAL IS DECIDED AGAINST THE STRUCTURE IN FORCE BEFORE THIS BAR, which is
// why structureFlipped is read before marketStructure is overwritten below.
//
// With marketStructure == 0 — no break seen yet — the first break in either
// direction is a break but NOT a change of character: it is the ESTABLISHMENT
// of structure, not a reversal of it. So structureFlipped stays false. That is
// the `marketStructure == -1` test, not a special case bolted on.
//
// The two conditions are independent `if` blocks, not `else if`, because
// crossedAbove and crossedBelow are genuinely independent: a bullish and a
// bearish break on the same bar would require lastSwingHigh < lastSwingLow,
// i.e. malformed structure. In that case both flags fire and structureFlipped
// ends up holding the second block's verdict, which is an arbitrary answer to a
// question the input data should not be able to ask. `else if` would instead
// silently resolve malformed structure in favour of the bull, which is worse —
// it looks like a real bearish break is being ignored.

if crossedAbove
    breakUp         := true
    structureFlipped := marketStructure == -1
    marketStructure  := 1

if crossedBelow
    breakDown       := true
    structureFlipped := marketStructure == 1
    marketStructure  := -1

// ─── Rendering: Swing Levels (spec 6.6) ─────────────────────────────────────
//
// TWO `var` LINE HANDLES FOR THE MODULE'S WHOLE LIFETIME, NOT ONE PER BAR.
//
// An earlier draft called line.new() whenever the last two swings were both
// non-na — true on nearly every bar after the first two pivots. That draws
// hundreds of overlapping horizontal lines, exhausts the max_lines_count budget
// shared with the Session Markers boundary lines, and renders a solid bar of
// colour rather than a readable level.
//
// Each handle is created once and afterwards only its RIGHT EDGE is moved with
// line.set_xy2(), so a stable level costs one drawing, not one per bar. Two
// handles exist, so live lines never exceed two — the spec 6.8 budget argument
// holds.
//
// THE PRICE COORDINATE IS LATCHED AT CREATION, because set_xy2() moves x2/y2
// and leaves y1 alone. That is correct for a level that has not changed, and
// WRONG for a new pivot at a different price: the line would keep drawing the
// old level while lastSwingHigh already holds the new one, so the chart and the
// break logic would disagree about where structure sits. The price is therefore
// compared against the line's own latched y1, and a changed price deletes and
// recreates the line. The delete frees the slot in the same execution, so the
// count is still bounded at two and the budget is untouched.

var line swingHighLine = na
var line swingLowLine  = na

if showSwingLevels
    if not na(lastSwingHigh)
        if na(swingHighLine) or lastSwingHigh != line.get_y1(swingHighLine)
            if not na(swingHighLine)
                line.delete(swingHighLine)
            swingHighLine := line.new(lastSwingHighBar, lastSwingHigh, bar_index, lastSwingHigh,
                 color=color.new(color.red, 45), style=line.style_dashed, width=1)
        else
            line.set_xy2(swingHighLine, bar_index, lastSwingHigh)

    if not na(lastSwingLow)
        if na(swingLowLine) or lastSwingLow != line.get_y1(swingLowLine)
            if not na(swingLowLine)
                line.delete(swingLowLine)
            swingLowLine := line.new(lastSwingLowBar, lastSwingLow, bar_index, lastSwingLow,
                 color=color.new(color.green, 45), style=line.style_dashed, width=1)
        else
            line.set_xy2(swingLowLine, bar_index, lastSwingLow)

// If the level toggle is switched off, the handles are dropped so re-enabling
// starts from a clean slate rather than resurrecting a stale line. Still two
// lines maximum, so the budget argument in spec 6.8 is unchanged.

else
    if not na(swingHighLine)
        line.delete(swingHighLine)
        swingHighLine := na
    if not na(swingLowLine)
        line.delete(swingLowLine)
        swingLowLine := na

// ─── Rendering: Break Markers (spec 6.6) ────────────────────────────────────
//
// One label per break, so this is inherently rare and does not compete for the
// max_labels_count budget the way the level lines once did for
// max_lines_count. Text is chosen from the TWO flags that already exist —
// never from a second pair of BoS/ChoCh booleans, which is the defect this
// module's interface exists to prevent.

if breakUp and showStructureBreaks
    label.new(bar_index, low, structureFlipped ? "ChoCh ▲" : "BoS ▲",
         style=label.style_label_up, color=color.new(color.green, 30),
         textcolor=color.white, size=size.normal)

if breakDown and showStructureBreaks
    label.new(bar_index, high, structureFlipped ? "ChoCh ▼" : "BoS ▼",
         style=label.style_label_down, color=color.new(color.red, 30),
         textcolor=color.white, size=size.normal)

// ─── Module Outputs (Signal Engine Interface, spec 6.7) ─────────────────────
//
//   breakUp           (bool) crossed above the last confirmed 1H swing high
//   breakDown         (bool) crossed below the last confirmed 1H swing low
//   structureFlipped  (bool) that break opposed the structure in force
//   marketStructure   (int)  1 bullish, -1 bearish, 0 not yet established
//
// marketStructure is a var and therefore persists across bars; the other three
// are recomputed each bar. A consumer that needs "structure turned bullish"
// tests `breakUp and structureFlipped` — NOT a separate bullishChoCh boolean,
// which would be true whenever breakUp is true in a downtrend and would
// double-count the event.
//
// Exactly these four. There is no bullishBoS / bullishChoCh pair, for the
// reason given in the header: ChoCh is a subset of BoS, and exporting both
// invites the confluence score in spec 7.3 to award 20 + 30 for a single event.
